\section{Training and new data}{

With a formula, the design is learned from the remote data, as
\code{design_matrix(formula, data)} learns it from a data frame:
\itemize{
\item Levels: those of an \code{ENUM} column (a factor in R) are its declared
levels; those of a character column are its distinct values in the rows
used, sorted as \code{\link[=factor]{factor()}} sorts them in R (not by SQL collation).
Logical columns are factors with levels \code{FALSE} and \code{TRUE}.
\item Rows: rows with a missing value (SQL \code{NULL}, or \code{NaN}) in any model
variable, response included, are dropped, as \code{\link[stats:na.omit]{stats::na.omit()}} drops
them.
\item Transformations whose parameters depend on the data, such as
\code{\link[stats:poly]{stats::poly()}}, \code{\link[splines:ns]{splines::ns()}} or \code{\link[=scale]{scale()}}, are fitted as
\code{\link[stats:model.frame]{stats::model.frame()}} fits them, on the whole of their input columns:
those columns (only those) are read into R once. To avoid this, fit
the specification in R, on a sample or on other data, and pass it
instead of the formula.
\item Variables computed by an R expression that yields a factor, such as
\code{factor(cyl)} or \code{interaction(a, b)}, take the levels that the
expression gives on the distinct values of its input columns.
}

With a specification (a design, a plan, a fitted model or another remote
design), the levels, contrasts and transformation parameters come from
it, and the remote data is only scored. Levels absent from the
specification raise the error that \code{\link[stats:model.frame]{stats::model.frame()}} raises. Rows
with missing values are dropped as well: the rows of the design are the
complete rows.
}

\section{Engines}{

\code{engine = "sql"} computes everything in DuckDB. It covers factors,
character and logical columns, numeric columns, interactions, and
numeric expressions built from \code{+}, \code{-}, \code{*}, \code{/}, \code{^}, \code{\link[=I]{I()}}, \code{\link[=log]{log()}}
(with base 2, 10 or a number), \code{\link[=log2]{log2()}}, \code{\link[=log10]{log10()}}, \code{\link[=exp]{exp()}}, \code{\link[=sqrt]{sqrt()}} and
\code{\link[=abs]{abs()}}. \code{engine = "chunked"} handles any formula: rows are read in
batches of \code{chunk_size} rows (from the table itself when it is a DuckDB
base table, otherwise from a temporary table holding the needed columns of
the rows used) and each batch goes through \code{design_matrix(spec, batch)}.
\code{engine = "auto"} (default) uses SQL when every variable can be
translated, and otherwise the chunked engine, with a message naming the
first term that cannot be translated. \code{\link[=design_explain]{design_explain()}} shows the
decision for each term, and the generated SQL.

In the chunked engine, a variable evaluated in R is taken as missing,
when selecting rows in SQL, if one of its input columns is missing; rows
where the R expression itself gives \code{NA} are then dropped in R. The
number of rows printed is that of the SQL selection until a pass over the
data has counted the rows kept.
}

\section{Operations}{

\itemize{
\item \code{crossprod(D)}: \verb{X'X}.
\item \code{crossprod(D, "y")}: \verb{X'y} for a column (or several columns) of the
remote data.
\item \code{\link[=design_moments]{design_moments()}}: \verb{X'X}, \verb{X'y}, \verb{y'y} and the number of rows in
one call, from the response of the formula by default, optionally
weighted by a column (\code{weights = "w"}): what (weighted) least squares
needs.
\item \code{colSums(D)}, \code{colMeans(D)}, and with \code{group = "g"}, the sums or means
within the groups of a column (sorted as \code{\link[base:rowsum]{base::rowsum()}} sorts them);
\code{weights = "w"} weights the rows.
\item \code{D \%*\% beta} or \code{\link[=design_predict]{design_predict()}}: the linear predictor, written to a
temporary view (SQL engine) or table (chunked engine) whose name is
returned.
\item \code{as.matrix(D)} collects the rows and builds the dense matrix, only when
there are at most \code{getOption("design.matrix.remote_max_rows", 1e5)} rows
or with \code{force = TRUE}.
\item \code{design_matrix(D, newdata)} applies the design to a data frame, and
\code{design_matrix(D, con, table = ...)} to other remote data.
}

Remote designs keep only the \emph{Algebra} promise of the package: results
agree with the in-memory computation up to floating point rounding. With
infinite values in the data, the placement of \code{NaN} and \code{Inf} in sums can
differ from the dense computation, and \code{NaN} may appear where the dense
result has \code{NA}.
}

\examples{
if (requireNamespace("duckdb", quietly = TRUE)) {
    con <- DBI::dbConnect(duckdb::duckdb())
    d <- data.frame(y = rnorm(100), x = rnorm(100), f = gl(4, 25))
    DBI::dbWriteTable(con, "d", d)
    D <- design_matrix(y ~ x * f, con, table = "d")
    D
    crossprod(D)
    m <- design_moments(D)
    solve(m$xtx, m$xty)
    p <- D \%*\% solve(m$xtx, m$xty)
    head(as.data.frame(p))
    DBI::dbDisconnect(con)
}
}
